data.content:
## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Classification

Your code contains:
| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on forced stop | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` blocking wait | Timeout enforcement | `Join(timeout)` or token-based |

### Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply does not work. The runtime no longer supports injecting exceptions into running threads. You **must** switch to cooperative cancellation.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException handler)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No need for ResetAbort — just let the loop exit naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2);

        _cts?.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        if (_workerThread is { IsAlive: true })
        {
            if (!_workerThread.Join(timeout.Value))
            {
                // Thread didn't stop in time — log a warning.
                // There is NO equivalent to Abort() in .NET 8.
                // If the thread is truly stuck, the process must exit
                // or you must redesign GetNextItem to respect the token.
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Critical Changes to Supporting Methods

Your `GetNextItem()` **must** accept and respect the token — otherwise cancellation stalls:

```csharp
// BEFORE: could block indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could break it
private WorkItem GetNextItem() { ... }

// AFTER: must throw OperationCanceledException when token fires
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // If using a BlockingCollection<T>:
    return _queue.Take(token);

    // If using a custom wait:
    token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(pollInterval); // replaces Thread.Sleep
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `GetNextItem()` blocks forever — `Cancel()` has no effect, thread never stops |
| **Using `Thread.Sleep` without token** | Sleep ignores cancellation. Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` instead |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Catch it only at the top-level loop; don't suppress it in inner methods |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Implement `IDisposable` on the host class |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It's cooperative — only fires at the next checkpoint. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive operations |
| **No fallback for truly stuck threads** | If `GetNextItem` calls unmanaged code that can't check the token, move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` |

---

### Summary of API Replacements

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|----------------|--------|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Remove — just `continue` or exit the loop |
| `Thread.Sleep(ms)` | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` |
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested` |

The fundamental shift: **you** are responsible for checking the token — the runtime will not inject an exception for you.
